## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply progressive hydration, per-column lazy-loading, and worker offloading to meet Lighthouse budgets in a heavy dashboard. Also probes SSR/CSR trade-offs and how to validate improvements.

## Key Concepts

- Progressive hydration
- Dynamic imports and code-splitting
- Virtualization
- Web Workers
- Lighthouse budgets and metrics
- IntersectionObserver for loading offscreen content

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example skeleton: dynamic import of heavy cell renderer
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';
const HeavyCell = dynamic(() => import('./cells/HeavyCell'));

// Progressive hydration with IntersectionObserver
const useColumnHydration = (columnRef) => {
  const [isHydrated, setIsHydrated] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          setIsHydrated(true);
          observer.disconnect();
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.1 }
    );

    if (columnRef.current) {
      observer.observe(columnRef.current);
    }

    return () => observer.disconnect();
  }, []);

  return isHydrated;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design high-frequency data handling in React with Redux and Context, focusing on minimizing re-renders during bursts while supporting multiple views. It probes knowledge of selectors, memoization, and rendering strategies in real-world data load scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Memoized selectors (reselect) for derived data
- React.memo for pure chart components
- useTransition to defer updates during bursts
- Virtualization/windowing to handle large bucket lists
- Redux data flow with high-rate streams and separate UI state in Context

## Code Example

```javascript
// Memoized selector sketch (conceptual)
import { createSelector } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const selectEvents = (state) => state.metrics.events; // raw events stream

const bucketizeBySecond = createSelector(
  [selectEvents],
  (events) => {
    const buckets = [];
    let current = [];
    let currentSecond = null;
    for (const e of events) {
      const t = Math.floor(e.ts / 1000) * 1000;
      if (currentSecond !== t) {
        if (current.length) buckets.push(current);
        current = [e];
        currentSecond = t;
      } else {
        current.push(e);
      }
    }
    if (current.length) buckets.push(current);
    return buckets;
  }
);
```

