## Solution Overview
Use closure to encapsulate rate limiter state with request queue and timer-based enforcement.

## Key Components
- Queue array for pending requests
- Processing flag to prevent race conditions
- Promise chaining for FIFO ordering
- Timer-based rate limit enforcement

## Implementation
```javascript
const createRateLimiter = (limit, interval) => {
  const queue = [];
  let processing = false;
  let lastRequest = 0;

  return async (request) => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      queue.push({ request, resolve, reject });
      processQueue();
    });
  };

  async function processQueue() {
    if (processing || queue.length === 0) return;
    processing = true;

    const now = Date.now();
    const timeSinceLastRequest = now - lastRequest;
    const delay = timeSinceLastRequest < interval ? interval - timeSinceLastRequest : 0;

    setTimeout(async () => {
      const { request, resolve, reject } = queue.shift();
      lastRequest = Date.now();

      try {
        const result = await request();
        resolve(result);
      } catch (error) {
        reject(error);
      }

      processing = false;
      processQueue();
    }, delay);
  }
};
```

## Benefits
- Maintains request order through FIFO queue
- Prevents rate limit violations with timing control
- Handles concurrent requests gracefully

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a small, practical caching layer with closures, per-URL keys, in-flight request sharing, and TTL-based invalidation using Promises. It mirrors common frontend API client patterns at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Closures to encapsulate cache state
- Per-URL Map storage with {promise, expires}
- Concurrency control: share in-flight Promise across callers
- TTL-based invalidation and error handling for retries
- fetch API and parsing JSON safely

## Code Example
```javascript
function makeApiClient() {
  const cache = new Map();
  return {
    fetchWithCache: (url, ttlMs) => {
      const now = Date.now();
      const entry = cache.get(url);
      if (entry && now < entry.expires) {
        return entry.promise;
      }
      const promise = fetch(url).then(res => {
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.statusText || res.status);
        return res.json();
      }).catch(err => {
        cache.delete(url);
        throw err;
      });
      cache.set(url, { promise, expires: ttlMs != null ? now + ttlMs : Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY });
      return promise;
    }
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to support AbortController cancellations and cleanup on navigation away?
- How would you test concurrent calls for the same URL ensuring a single network request is issued?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine REST fetch, WebSocket, and Service Worker offline strategies in a practical, real-world scenario.

## Key Concepts
- **DOM updates**: Append-only rendering to avoid duplicate display
- **Fetch**: Initial payload load for historical messages
- **WebSocket**: Real-time message streaming
- **Service Worker**: Offline caching for assets and payload
- **Deduplication**: Using a Set to track seen messageId values

## Code Examples

### REST Fetch
```javascript
async function loadMessages(roomId) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/messages?roomId=${roomId}&limit=20`);
  const messages = await res.json();
  messages.forEach(msg => addMessage(msg));
}
```

### WebSocket Handling
```javascript
const ws = new WebSocket(`ws://api.example.com/chat/${roomId}`);
ws.onmessage = (event) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(event.data);
  if (!seenIds.has(msg.id)) {
    seenIds.add(msg.id);
    addMessage(msg);
  }
};
```

### Service Worker Cache Strategy
```javascript
// sw.js
self.addEventListener('fetch', (e) => {
  if (e.request.url.includes('/api/messages')) {
    e.respondWith(
      caches.open('chat-cache').then(cache => 
        cache.match(e.request).then(res => res || fetch(e.request))
      )
    );
  }
});
```

### Minimal Render Function
```javascript
const seenIds = new Set();
function addMessage(msg) {
  if (seenIds.has(msg.id)) return;
  seenIds.add(msg.id);
  const el = document.createElement('div');
  el.textContent = msg.text;
  document.getElementById('chat').appendChild(el);
}
```

